course: biology
lesson: 392
title: Poisonous plants to stay away from: berries, foxglove, yew and nightshade
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In the park you spot a bush covered in shiny berries, red and juicy like sweets. They look delicious, but some of the most dangerous plants in Europe have the prettiest berries.
explain: Plants cannot run away from animals that want to eat them, so many protect their leaves, seeds and roots with poisons. A poison that only makes a deer feel sick can be very dangerous for a small child.
explain: Colour, smell and birds are no guide: there are poisonous berries of every colour, some poisonous flowers smell sweet, and birds can eat berries that would harm you.
explain: So there is one golden rule: never eat any wild berry, leaf, seed or mushroom unless a grown-up who really knows plants says it is safe. And if a little child eats something wild, tell a grown-up at once.
fact: Many berries that look tasty are poisonous, and some are dangerous even in small amounts; plants use poisons to protect their leaves and seeds.
fact: The golden rule: never eat any wild berry, leaf, seed or mushroom unless a grown-up who really knows plants says it is safe, and tell a grown-up at once if someone has eaten one.
fact: Almost every part of the yew tree is poisonous, especially its needles and the hard seed inside the red cup; birds eat the red flesh and pass the seed out whole.
fact: Yews can live well over a thousand years, and some in British churchyards are among the oldest trees in Europe.
fact: Foxgloves, tall spikes of purple finger-shaped bells, are poisonous because chemicals in them change how the heart beats; the same chemicals, carefully measured by doctors, became an old heart medicine.
fact: Deadly nightshade, with shiny black berries on green star-shaped cups, is one of the most poisonous plants in Europe; its poison affects the nerves, heart and eyes.
fact: Deadly nightshade's other name, belladonna, means beautiful lady in Italian, from an old and dangerous use of its juice on the eyes.
fact: Lily of the valley smells sweet but every part is poisonous, including its red autumn berries, and its leaves can be mixed up with wild garlic.
fact: Daffodil bulbs are poisonous and have made people sick when mixed up with onions, so bulbs and onions should be kept apart.
fact: Potato and tomato plants are relatives of nightshade; their leaves, stems and green potato fruits are poisonous, and potatoes turned green by light carry more of the poison, so they are kept in the dark.
fact: Sosnowsky's hogweed and giant hogweed, huge plants with white flower umbrellas, have sap that makes skin burn and blister in sunlight; Sosnowsky's hogweed has spread across Ukraine from old farm fields.
fact: Apple seeds hold a chemical that can turn into poison, but swallowing a few by accident is harmless, because the hard seed coat usually passes through you whole.
fact: There are poisonous berries of every colour, and birds do not react to the same poisons as people, so neither colour nor watching birds can tell you a berry is safe.
exercise: What is the golden rule about wild berries? | Never eat one unless a grown-up who really knows plants says it is safe | Eat only the red ones, because red means a berry is safe | Eat them if you have seen birds eating them too | Eat only a tiny taste first to check that they are safe
exercise: Why do many plants contain poisons? | To protect their leaves and seeds from being eaten | To make their flowers smell nicer to the bees | To help them grow taller than other plants | To keep their leaves green in winter
exercise: A little child has eaten a berry from the park. What should you do? | Tell a grown-up at once | Wait and see if the child feels ill | Give the child more berries to compare | Keep it a secret so nobody is cross
exercise: Which part of the yew is especially poisonous? | The needles and the hard seed inside the red cup | Only the rough bark on the oldest trees | Only the roots deep under the ground | No part at all, the yew is a safe tree to chew
exercise: Where do some of the oldest yews in Europe grow? | In British churchyards | In the oases of the Sahara | On frozen Arctic islands | In big city flower pots
exercise: How does the foxglove poison harm people? | It changes how the heart beats | It turns the skin blue for good | It makes all your hair fall out | It only ever causes a small sneeze
exercise: What did doctors make from foxglove chemicals, carefully measured? | An old heart medicine | A soothing cough sweet | A strong summer sun cream | A bright purple hair dye
exercise: What does deadly nightshade look like? | Shiny black berries sitting on green star-shaped cups | Big round red apples with little white spots | Tiny white bell flowers that smell sweet | Tall purple bells shaped like the fingers of a glove
exercise: What does belladonna mean? | Beautiful lady | Deadly berry | Black flower | Night shadow
exercise: Which part of lily of the valley is poisonous? | Every part, including its red berries | Only the white flowers, the rest is safe to eat | Only the roots under the soil | No part at all, it is safe to eat
exercise: Which food can daffodil bulbs be mixed up with? | Onions | Carrots | Apples | Potatoes
exercise: Which parts of a potato plant are poisonous? | The leaves, stems and green fruits | Only the potatoes that we cook and eat | Only the flowers | None, the whole plant is perfectly safe
exercise: Why should potatoes be kept in a dark cupboard? | Light turns them green and they make more poison | Dark makes them grow much bigger while they wait to be cooked | Light makes them go soft and very sweet | Mice cannot find them in the dark
exercise: What does hogweed sap do to skin? | Makes it burn and blister in sunlight | Makes it very shiny and soft, like a cream | Turns it bright green for about a week | Makes it cold and numb
exercise: What happens if you swallow a few apple seeds by accident? | Nothing harmful, they usually pass through you whole | You get very ill at once and must go to hospital | A small apple tree starts growing inside you | Your stomach turns sour for a year
exercise: Can the colour of a berry tell you if it is safe? | No, there are poisonous berries of every colour | Yes, all red berries are safe to eat | Yes, all white berries are safe | Yes, all black berries are safe to eat
test: «birds eat them berries all day, so they're fine for us» What is the best correction? | Birds do not react to the same poisons as people, so that proves nothing | Right, birds and people have the same stomachs, so they can eat the same things | Right, but only if you have seen two birds eat them | Birds die from eating berries all the time
test: Leo says apple seeds are deadly poison and he's dying because he ate two. What is true? | Swallowing a few by accident is harmless | He needs to go to hospital at once in an ambulance | Apple seeds are safer to eat than the apple | Apple seeds are a kind of strong medicine
test: In a fairy tale, a witch hides poison in a shiny red apple. Which real plant has bright red cups hiding a poisonous seed? | The yew | The apple tree | The rose | The old oak
test: «ugh, potato's gone green on one side, whatever, I'll just boil it» What is the best advice? | Cut the green parts away or throw it out, green parts carry more poison | Green potatoes are the healthiest kind, so eat it all, skin and all | Boiling turns green potatoes into sweet ones | Green means it is ripe and ready to eat
test: A giant plant taller than your dad with huge white flower umbrellas grows near the playground. What should you do? | Do not touch it and tell a grown-up | Pick a big flower to show your friends | Hide inside its big leaves in the sun | Snap a stem to see what the sap looks like
test: What would happen if someone picked lily of the valley leaves thinking they were wild garlic and ate them? | They could be poisoned, because lily of the valley is poisonous | Nothing at all, because both of them are safe and tasty salad leaves | They would just taste of garlic | They would get stronger hearts from it
test: «my nan says foxglove is a heart medicine, so it's healthy innit» What is the best answer? | Doctors measure the chemicals carefully; the plant itself is poisonous | Yes, eating a few fresh foxglove flowers every day is good for your heart | No, foxglove has nothing to do with the heart | Yes, but only the purple ones are healthy
test: Count them: a foxglove spike carries 24 bells and a second one carries 18. How many bells are there to look at but never put in your mouth? | 42 | 32 | 44 | 36
test: In a cartoon, a bear stuffs its mouth with every berry in the forest and is fine. What is wrong for a child? | Many wild berries are poisonous for people, so never eat them without a knowing grown-up | Nothing is wrong, because all the berries in a forest are safe if a bear eats them first | Only cartoon berries are poisonous | Bears are the only animals that berries can harm
test: «white berries are always bad, others are fine, simple» Is this a safe rule? | No, there are poisonous berries of every colour | Yes, because only the white berries are poisonous | Yes, red and black berries are always safe to eat | Yes, it is the rule doctors use
test: Why are poisonous berries that look sweet so dangerous for toddlers? | Toddlers put pretty things in their mouths and are small, so less poison harms them | Toddlers can always taste poison and spit it out | Berries are only poisonous to grown-ups and never to small children with tiny tummies | Toddlers are protected by milk
test: Your mum stores onions and daffodil bulbs in the same box in the shed. Why is that a bad idea? | Someone could mix them up and cook a poisonous bulb | The bulbs make the onions grow yellow flowers in the box | The onions make the bulbs rot at once | Daffodils steal the onion smell
exam: Select the response consistent with the established rule concerning the consumption of wild berries, leaves and seeds. | None shall be consumed unless a competent adult with knowledge of plants has confirmed that it is safe | Berries of a red colour may be consumed without restriction, red being a reliable indication of safety | Any berry consumed by birds may be presumed safe for human beings | A small quantity may be tasted in order to ascertain its safety
exam: Identify the principal reason for which numerous plants contain poisonous substances. | To deter animals from consuming their leaves and seeds | To attract animals to consume them in the greatest possible quantity | To accelerate the growth of their stems | To preserve the colour of their flowers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the accidental swallowing of a small number of apple seeds? | It is harmless, as the hard seed coat ordinarily passes through the body intact | It invariably results in grave illness requiring immediate admission to hospital | It results in the germination of a tree within the stomach | It confers protection against all other poisons
exam: Ascertain the reason for which potatoes are customarily stored in darkness. | Exposure to light causes them to turn green and to produce a greater quantity of poison | Darkness causes them to double in size | Exposure to light renders them excessively sweet and unfit for the preparation of any meal | Darkness prevents their consumption by birds
